;;; -*- Mode:Lisp -*-
;;; README.abclr2
;;; An example program: Control of Parallelism.

#|
This example program is controlling of explosion of parallelism.
Number of object created is suppressed by meta-level scheduling.

For detailed mechanisms, please refer following article:
	H. Masuhara, S. Matsuoka, T. Watanabe, and A. Yonezawa,
	Object-Oriented Concurrent Reflective Languages can be
	Implemented Efficiently,
	in Proceedings of OOPSLA '92 (to appear).

To run this example, incorporate file "start" first.  Then incorporate
either file "fib" or file "qsort".

File "fib" is for the computation of Fibonacci numbers.  An expression
[test-pc <== [:start N]] starts the computation of Nth Fibonacci
number with priotirty based scheduling.  On the other hand, an
expression [test-np <== [:start N]] starts the computation with simple
scheduling (FIFO manner).  Each computation creates log files named
"{active,object}-{pc,np}-N". (The first word means that the number of
active objects (objects which has non-processed messages) or the
number of existing objects are counted; the second word means the
scheduling policy.)

File "qsort" is for the Quicksorting.  Before starting computation, an
expression [init <= N] must be executed: this sets up size N
randomized array for sorting on variable a.  Expressions
 [test-pc <== [:start a N]] or [test-np <== [:start a N]]
starts quicksorting with priority-based/FIFO-based scheduling.  These
computation also generates the log files which names are given by the
same manner of Fibonnaci comuptations'.

Unfortunately, these programs are very time consumptive.  Please be
careful not to run with big numbers.

;;
(inc "start")
(inc "fib")
[test-pc <== [:start 10]]
[test-np <== [:start 10]]
;; files active-pc-10, active-np-10, object-pc-10, and object-np-10
;; are created.

(inc "qsort")
[init <= 10]
[test-pc <== [:start a 10]]
[test-np <== [:start a 10]]
;; ditto
|#